1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,497
September 1967.

2
00:00:01,497 --> 00:00:07,548
A three year old Appaloosa mare walks out into a pasture in southern Colorado.

3
00:00:07,548 --> 00:00:13,366
Two days later she is found dead, and her head is picked clean to the bone.

4
00:00:13,833 --> 00:00:18,474
Newspapers across America said she was killed by a flying saucer.

5
00:00:18,474 --> 00:00:22,615
A pathologist said the cuts were too clean for any animal.

6
00:00:22,615 --> 00:00:24,899
A sheriff said it was lightning.

7
00:00:25,366 --> 00:00:28,170
So tonight we open this as an inquest.

8
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:29,129
Six exhibits.

9
00:00:29,129 --> 00:00:32,966
Every claim called as a witness, and cross examined.

10
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:34,616
Who was the horse?

11
00:00:34,616 --> 00:00:36,128
What exactly was found?

12
00:00:36,128 --> 00:00:37,705
What do the experts say?

13
00:00:37,705 --> 00:00:42,766
And why did a few hundred cattle in the years after seem to die the same way?

14
00:00:43,233 --> 00:00:44,786
At the end, a verdict.

15
00:00:44,786 --> 00:00:49,587
Unexplained, explained, or a legend that grew faster than the facts.

16
00:00:49,587 --> 00:00:50,999
Court is in session.

17
00:00:55,466 --> 00:00:56,233
Exhibit A.

18
00:00:56,233 --> 00:00:57,077
The victim.

19
00:00:57,077 --> 00:00:58,458
Her name was Lady.

20
00:00:58,458 --> 00:01:06,666
A young Appaloosa mare, owned by Nellie Lewis, and pastured on her brother Harry King's ranch near Alamosa.

21
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:16,566
The King ranch sits in the San Luis Valley, a high flat basin more than seven thousand feet up, ringed by the Sangre de Cristo mountains.

22
00:01:17,033 --> 00:01:20,290
Harry King and his wife Agnes ran cattle there.

23
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:23,338
In early September the mare did not come in.

24
00:01:23,338 --> 00:01:25,833
On the seventh, she was simply gone.

25
00:01:26,299 --> 00:01:28,122
They searched the pasture.

26
00:01:28,122 --> 00:01:28,683
Nothing.

27
00:01:28,683 --> 00:01:31,136
The grass was dry, the ground firm.

28
00:01:31,136 --> 00:01:36,533
Two days later, on September ninth, Agnes and Harry walked out and found her.

29
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,079
She lay on her side in a patch of flattened brush.

30
00:01:40,079 --> 00:01:45,500
And from a distance, Harry said later, it looked like something was wrong with her head.

31
00:01:45,966 --> 00:01:48,206
The Pueblo Chieftain ran the story.

32
00:01:48,206 --> 00:01:50,190
The wire services picked it up.

33
00:01:50,190 --> 00:01:53,966
And somewhere along the way the horse got a different name.

34
00:01:54,433 --> 00:01:56,392
Reporters called her Snippy.

35
00:01:56,392 --> 00:02:01,218
That was actually the name of her sire, a horse owned by Berle Lewis.

36
00:02:01,218 --> 00:02:04,366
The wrong name stuck, and so did the mystery.

37
00:02:04,833 --> 00:02:05,507
Exhibit B.

38
00:02:05,507 --> 00:02:06,114
The body.

39
00:02:06,114 --> 00:02:13,666
The head and neck were stripped of flesh, down to clean white bone, as if cut away along a line at the shoulder.

40
00:02:14,133 --> 00:02:16,427
The rest of the body was intact.

41
00:02:16,427 --> 00:02:18,076
No blood on the ground.

42
00:02:18,076 --> 00:02:19,080
No drag marks.

43
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:23,166
No tracks of people or vehicles, according to the family.

44
00:02:23,633 --> 00:02:32,259
Around her, six round marks in the ground, about two inches across and four inches deep, set in a circle roughly three feet wide.

45
00:02:32,259 --> 00:02:34,800
The brush around her was pressed flat.

46
00:02:35,266 --> 00:02:41,280
A forest service man, Duane Martin, reportedly passed a Geiger counter over the spot.

47
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:45,666
The needle, he said, read higher near the body than elsewhere.

48
00:02:46,133 --> 00:02:49,008
That detail would be repeated for decades.

49
00:02:49,008 --> 00:02:55,033
It also came from a handheld instrument, a single reading, and no record of calibration.

50
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:57,058
And then people came.

51
00:02:57,058 --> 00:02:58,320
Carloads of them.

52
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:00,696
Reporters, sightseers, ufo fans.

53
00:03:00,696 --> 00:03:05,966
By the time anyone competent looked, the ground had been trampled flat.

54
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:10,973
Locals also talked of strange lights over the valley that autumn.

55
00:03:10,973 --> 00:03:15,933
The flying saucer story had arrived, and it fit the shape of the times.

56
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:17,134
Exhibit C.

57
00:03:17,134 --> 00:03:18,015
The sheriff.

58
00:03:18,015 --> 00:03:23,375
Ben Phillips of Alamosa County looked at the case and gave a flat answer.

59
00:03:23,375 --> 00:03:26,533
The mare, he said, was struck by lightning.

60
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:28,852
Lightning does kill horses.

61
00:03:28,852 --> 00:03:32,969
It leaves burns on the skin and sometimes singes the ground.

62
00:03:32,969 --> 00:03:35,508
It does not strip a head to the bone.

63
00:03:35,508 --> 00:03:37,566
And he never visited the site.

64
00:03:38,033 --> 00:03:41,646
Then came Doctor John Altshuler, a Denver pathologist.

65
00:03:41,646 --> 00:03:47,400
He looked at the remains years later, and his account became the centre of the legend.

66
00:03:47,866 --> 00:03:53,630
He said the cut at the neck was clean, almost cauterized, like a surgical incision.

67
00:03:53,630 --> 00:03:59,533
He said the heart, lungs and thyroid were gone, and the brain looked unusually clean.

68
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,715
In 1967 the Associated Press quoted him saying organs were missing, and that it was definitely not lightning.

69
00:04:08,715 --> 00:04:12,233
His later, bigger claims came decades after.

70
00:04:12,699 --> 00:04:14,093
Now the other side.

71
00:04:14,093 --> 00:04:24,066
An independent veterinary pathologist for the University of Colorado, working for the Air Force project, saw the same horse differently.

72
00:04:24,533 --> 00:04:31,876
The government project was run by physicist Edward Condon at the University of Colorado.

73
00:04:31,876 --> 00:04:37,133
Snippy is Case thirty two in its final report, written in 1968.

74
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:42,242
Let's hear what the Condon team's own investigators actually found.

75
00:04:42,242 --> 00:04:46,399
Because it is not what the flying saucer version leaves out.

76
00:04:46,866 --> 00:04:47,645
Exhibit D.

77
00:04:47,645 --> 00:04:49,357
The cross examination.

78
00:04:49,357 --> 00:04:54,184
Investigator Wadsworth Ayer reached the case after the crowds.

79
00:04:54,184 --> 00:04:58,466
First fact: hundreds of visitors had trampled the site.

80
00:04:58,933 --> 00:04:59,791
Second fact.

81
00:04:59,791 --> 00:05:03,296
The vet found a severe infection in one hind leg.

82
00:05:03,296 --> 00:05:08,733
The mare may have been lame, sick and in pain for some time before she died.

83
00:05:09,199 --> 00:05:09,933
Third fact.

84
00:05:09,933 --> 00:05:11,533
A knife cut in the neck.

85
00:05:11,533 --> 00:05:18,133
His working idea: someone may have found her suffering and put her down, which is legal on a ranch.

86
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:21,123
Fourth fact, and the strangest one.

87
00:05:21,123 --> 00:05:21,700
Magpies.

88
00:05:21,700 --> 00:05:28,766
Those birds will strip soft flesh from a carcass in days, and they favour an opened neck or wound.

89
00:05:29,233 --> 00:05:31,971
The skin and muscle go first from the head.

90
00:05:31,971 --> 00:05:37,066
What you are left with is a clean, pale skull that can look cut from a distance.

91
00:05:37,533 --> 00:05:46,857
As for the missing organs: the investigators said that in a body found days after death, the soft organs are often gone or rotted.

92
00:05:46,857 --> 00:05:47,933
That is normal.

93
00:05:48,399 --> 00:05:50,770
The pressed brush and six round marks?

94
00:05:50,770 --> 00:05:57,633
The team noted that the horse's body, and its struggles, could flatten brush, and the marks were not specific.

95
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:01,686
Their conclusion in the Condon report was blunt.

96
00:06:01,686 --> 00:06:04,002
No evidence of abnormal causes.

97
00:06:04,002 --> 00:06:08,933
No evidence of anything beyond predators, decay, and a sick horse.

98
00:06:09,399 --> 00:06:10,827
Does that settle it?

99
00:06:10,827 --> 00:06:11,540
Not quite.

100
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:16,036
Doctor Altshuler's own account still disagrees with the report.

101
00:06:16,036 --> 00:06:20,033
Two honest accounts, decades apart, of the same remains.

102
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:24,332
And that, says every honest researcher, is the problem.

103
00:06:24,332 --> 00:06:30,533
The one body that really mattered was gone, buried, and never tested to modern standards.

104
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:31,773
Exhibit E.

105
00:06:31,773 --> 00:06:32,547
The panic.

106
00:06:32,547 --> 00:06:42,066
Within weeks, ranchers across Colorado, Kansas, Nebraska and the Dakotas began to report cattle found dead, oddly stripped.

107
00:06:42,533 --> 00:06:48,928
Some said ears, tongues, udders and sex organs were removed, with no blood and no tracks.

108
00:06:48,928 --> 00:06:52,233
Whole towns started watching the sky at night.

109
00:06:52,699 --> 00:06:54,816
Farmers sat up with rifles.

110
00:06:54,816 --> 00:06:56,697
Sheriffs formed patrols.

111
00:06:56,697 --> 00:07:02,733
A Colorado state official called the cases a wave of unexplained mutilations.

112
00:07:03,199 --> 00:07:05,484
Black helicopters were reported.

113
00:07:05,484 --> 00:07:06,555
Strange lights.

114
00:07:06,555 --> 00:07:07,554
Unmarked vans.

115
00:07:07,554 --> 00:07:11,766
The story grew a government villain, and then an alien one.

116
00:07:12,233 --> 00:07:20,166
By the nineteen seventies the Federal Bureau of Investigation and state police had hundreds of reports on their desks.

117
00:07:20,633 --> 00:07:27,820
In nineteen seventy nine the FBI's Kenneth Rommel investigated New Mexico cases for the Senate.

118
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:31,300
His team put the cause as predators and decay.

119
00:07:31,766 --> 00:07:36,067
Scavengers can take eyes, tongues, lips and soft parts first.

120
00:07:36,067 --> 00:07:41,566
And blowflies soften skin edges, so the cuts can look like a scalpel did them.

121
00:07:42,033 --> 00:07:52,833
Researchers who studied the cases, including a dedicated Colorado investigation, found no pattern that predators and ordinary decay could not explain.

122
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:54,053
Exhibit F.

123
00:07:54,053 --> 00:07:55,032
The theories.

124
00:07:55,032 --> 00:08:03,166
Theory one, the popular one: extraterrestrials harvesting tissue from Earth animals, for study, or for food.

125
00:08:03,633 --> 00:08:08,070
Theory two: satanic cults, performing rituals on animals.

126
00:08:08,070 --> 00:08:09,939
A real panic of the era.

127
00:08:09,939 --> 00:08:15,466
Investigators found no cult, no evidence, no arrests tied to the cases.

128
00:08:15,933 --> 00:08:23,355
Theory three: a secret government programme testing something on cattle, from germ warfare to radiation.

129
00:08:23,355 --> 00:08:24,282
Rumours only.

130
00:08:24,282 --> 00:08:26,566
No documents have ever shown it.

131
00:08:27,033 --> 00:08:28,559
Theory four: fallout.

132
00:08:28,559 --> 00:08:35,900
Cold war nuclear testing worried farmers everywhere, and a Geiger counter in a story felt like proof.

133
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:36,700
It was not.

134
00:08:37,166 --> 00:08:40,244
Theory five, the quiet one: nothing happened.

135
00:08:40,244 --> 00:08:47,700
Normal deaths, normal predators, and an alarmed public primed by the saucer craze to read patterns into them.

136
00:08:48,166 --> 00:08:51,240
Social scientists call it a moral panic.

137
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:54,775
Reports surge after a famous story, then fade.

138
00:08:54,775 --> 00:08:59,233
The mutilation wave rose and fell with newspaper coverage.

139
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:01,563
And the legend never died.

140
00:09:01,563 --> 00:09:11,166
In the San Luis Valley a roadside tourist attraction still draws visitors, and the story was revived in the news in twenty twenty two.

141
00:09:11,633 --> 00:09:13,603
The court has heard everything.

142
00:09:13,603 --> 00:09:18,433
Time to weigh the evidence, exhibit by exhibit, and give the case its score.

143
00:09:18,899 --> 00:09:23,268
The inquest asks the same three questions about each claim.

144
00:09:23,268 --> 00:09:24,527
Is it documented?

145
00:09:24,527 --> 00:09:25,860
Is it independent?

146
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:28,600
And does the simpler explanation fit?

147
00:09:29,066 --> 00:09:31,505
First, the part we cannot answer.

148
00:09:31,505 --> 00:09:35,938
We never saw the original data from Altshuler's examination.

149
00:09:35,938 --> 00:09:37,712
The remains were buried.

150
00:09:37,712 --> 00:09:39,633
No tissue was ever stored.

151
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:42,092
Second, the fact that matters.

152
00:09:42,092 --> 00:09:49,466
The one place where the legend started, Lady, has a plain explanation that fits every observation we can check.

153
00:09:49,933 --> 00:09:51,371
Remember what she was.

154
00:09:51,371 --> 00:09:58,433
A sick young horse, in pain, on a ranch where someone may have ended her suffering, and nature did the rest.

155
00:09:58,899 --> 00:10:01,160
Why does it feel so unsatisfying?

156
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:07,600
Because a plain explanation arrives long after the exciting one, and never gets the headlines.

157
00:10:08,066 --> 00:10:08,568
Debrief.

158
00:10:08,568 --> 00:10:11,266
Time to score the case of Snippy the horse.

159
00:10:11,733 --> 00:10:18,776
A real horse, found dead in September nineteen sixty seven, her head stripped to bone: evidence.

160
00:10:18,776 --> 00:10:23,838
Magpies, infection, and a knife cut: documented in the Condon report.

161
00:10:23,838 --> 00:10:27,433
Trampled site and one early Geiger reading: weak.

162
00:10:27,899 --> 00:10:30,082
Alien surgery: no evidence.

163
00:10:30,082 --> 00:10:33,721
A cult or secret government lab: no evidence.

164
00:10:33,721 --> 00:10:39,380
A national wave of mutilations as a news driven panic: well supported.

165
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:43,099
One honest unknown, the lost original remains.

166
00:10:43,566 --> 00:10:44,410
The verdict.

167
00:10:44,410 --> 00:10:47,363
Explained, as far as the evidence reaches.

168
00:10:47,363 --> 00:10:49,964
A sick horse, scavengers and a panic.

169
00:10:49,964 --> 00:10:52,073
The saucers were never needed.

170
00:10:52,073 --> 00:10:55,166
But the buried truth keeps the legend alive.

171
00:10:55,633 --> 00:11:02,515
Somewhere in Colorado, a family lost a horse and woke up the next day in the middle of a national story.

172
00:11:02,515 --> 00:11:04,566
They never asked for any of it.

173
00:11:05,033 --> 00:11:12,359
Next case: one night, one radio telescope, and a signal so strong the astronomer wrote one word beside it.

174
00:11:12,359 --> 00:11:15,399
Was it the first message from another world?

175
00:11:15,866 --> 00:11:17,935
What do you think happened to Lady?

176
00:11:17,935 --> 00:11:22,899
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
